{"id":"review-deadlines-by-station","title":"Which review desks miss the City’s 2025 benchmark","question":"At each review station, how often does a building permit review finish within the 2025 City performance target?","finding":"Among 127,033 finished first station reviews of permits filed in 2015–2026, 77% took more than 30 calendar days from filing. Of 88,730 finished rechecks, 18% exceeded 14 calendar days. The City announced these performance targets in May 2025. Among stations with 1,000 or more first reviews, Structural plan check fell within the benchmark least often (17%) and Mechanical and electrical plan check most often (43%). Building plan check met it on 19% of first reviews and the Planning zoning check on 23%. 4,965 review steps on permits filed in these years have no finish date yet.","how":"Every row of the City’s review-metrics table for permits filed from 2015: one row per visit to a station. \"Met\" is the table’s own flag (met_cal_sla). For a first review the table counts days from the filing date; \"station days\" here are arrival at the station to finish. Stations with fewer than 50 finished reviews are left out.","limits":"PermitSF announced these targets in May 2025. Earlier rows are retrospective comparisons. The City set a goal of meeting each calendar-day target on 75% of applicable reviews. Its April 2024 housing guidance used business days, different start events and unit-count thresholds. The City’s first-review clock starts at filing, so a station reached after others (water, street use) can exceed the benchmark because of time spent elsewhere. A review can be paused while the applicant revises plans, and the table does not say whose wait it was. The deadline is the departments’ own service target, not a legal limit.","tier":"OFFICIAL","columns":[{"k":"station","t":"Review station","type":"text"},{"k":"dept","t":"Department","type":"text"},{"k":"first","t":"First reviews","type":"int"},{"k":"firstMet","t":"Met 30-day benchmark","ty